ion":"2025-11-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-11-20","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-20","firstIndexDate":"2025-11-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1225718","source":"PPR","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PPR1225718"]},"doi":"10.64898/2026.01.20.700586","title":"Integration-coupled activation of promoterless combinatorial pathway libraries in  <i>Clostridium</i>  avoids burden during DNA assembly","authorString":"Mordaka PM, Williamson J, Heap JT.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mordaka PM","lastName":"Mordaka","firstName":"Pawel","initials":"PM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Imperial College Centre for Synthetic Biology, Department of Life Sciences, , ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Williamson J","lastName":"Williamson","firstName":"James","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Life Sciences, Biodiscovery Institute, , University Park , , NG7 2RD ,"}]}},{"fullName":"Heap JT","lastName":"Heap","firstName":"John","initials":"JT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Imperial College Centre for Synthetic Biology, Department of Life Sciences, , ,"}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Combinatorial DNA design and assembly is an efficient and pragmatic way to obtain high-performing metabolic pathway designs quickly. However, implementation may require organism-specific technical barriers to be overcome. Firstly, suitable expression control parts such as promoters and ribosome-binding sites (RBSs) which provide a suitable range of expression levels need to be identified or developed. Secondly, these need to be assembled into pathway-encoding combinatorial libraries of sufficient size, quality and diversity. For organisms with transformation frequencies too low to allow direct transformation of library assembly reactions, such as many  Clostridium spp., assembly and amplification is typically carried out using  Escherichia coli . However, if constructs are deleterious (or ‘burdensome’) to  E. coli , which is often the case when using  Clostridium genetic parts, poor libraries may be obtained. Here we develop a new approach called integration-coupled activation of promoterless sequences (ICAPS) to overcome this barrier and therefore enable combinatorial assembly in  Clostridium . Libraries were designed and assembled as promoterless synthetic operons, preventing expression during DNA assembly, and expression was only activated later, when constructs were integrated into the host genome downstream of a promoter. Variation of expression levels was achieved using a range of context-resistant RBS sequences. This approach was used to produce a  Clostridium acetobutylicum library with combinatorial expression variants of an introduced hexanol pathway. This proof of concept provides a generally-applicable approach to implement combinatorial metabolic pathway-encoding libraries in  Clostridium spp., circumventing the excessive strength of  Clostridium expression control parts in  E. coli , and is applicable to other organisms.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Biotechnology and Biological Sciences Research Council","grantId":"BB/M002454/1","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Biotechnology and Biological Sciences Research Council","grantId":"BB/V001396/1","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.01.20.700586"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/article/PPR/PPR1225718"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/api/fulltextRepo?pprId=PPR1225718&type=FILE&fileName=EMS214357-pdf.pdf&mimeType=application/pdf"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"Y","nihAuthMan":"N","manuscriptId":"EMS214357","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"40107619","source":"MED","pmid":"40107619","pmcid":"PMC12018979","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12018979"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.jbc.2025.108410","title":"Cryo-EM structures define the electron bifurcating flavobicluster and ferredoxin binding site in an archaeal Nfn-Bfu transhydrogenase.","authorString":"Xiao X, Schut GJ, Feng X, Nguyen DMN, Huang H, Wang S, Li H, Adams MWW.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xiao X","lastName":"Xiao","firstName":"Xiansha","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Structural Biology, Van Andel Institute, Grand Rapids, Michigan, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Schut GJ","lastName":"Schut","firstName":"Gerrit J","initials":"GJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Feng X","lastName":"Feng","firstName":"Xiang","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Structural Biology, Van Andel Institute, Grand Rapids, Michigan, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Nguyen DMN","lastName":"Nguyen","firstName":"Diep M N","initials":"DMN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang H","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Haiyan","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang S","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Shuning","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1166-078X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Li H","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Huilin","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Structural Biology, Van Andel Institute, Grand Rapids, Michigan, USA. Electronic address: Huilin.Li@vai.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Adams MWW","lastName":"Adams","firstName":"Michael W W","initials":"MWW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, University of Georgia, Athens, Georgia, USA. Electronic address: adamsm@uga.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1166-078X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"301","journalIssueId":3942488,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","nlmid":"2985121R","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-04-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"108410","abstractText":"Flavin-based electron bifurcation couples exergonic and endergonic redox reactions in one enzyme complex to circumvent thermodynamic barriers and minimize free energy loss. Two unrelated enzymes designated NfnSL and NfnABC catalyze the NADPH-dependent reduction of ferredoxin and NAD. Bifurcation by NfnSL resides with a single FAD but the bifurcation mechanism of NfnABC, which represents the diverse and ubiquitous Bfu enzyme family, is completely different and largely unknown. Using cryo-EM structures of an archaeal NfnABC, we show that its bifurcation site is a flavobicluster consisting of FMN, one [4Fe-4S] and one [2Fe-2S] cluster where zinc atoms replace two additional clusters previously identified in other Bfu enzymes. NADH binds to the flavobicluster site of NfnABC and induces conformational changes that allow ferredoxin to bind between the C-terminal domains of NfnC and NfnB. Site-directed mutational analyses support the proposed mechanism that is likely conserved in all members of the Bfu enzyme family.","affiliation":"Department of Structural Biology, Van Andel Institute, Grand Rapids, Michigan, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Van Andel Institute","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Chemical Sciences, Geosciences, and Biosciences Division","orderIn":0},{"agency":"US Department of Energy","grantId":"DE-FG02-95ER20175","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Basic Energy Sciences","orderIn":0},{"agency":"US Department of Energy","grantId":"DE-SC0020085","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Archaeal Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Ferredoxins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cryoelectron Microscopy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Binding Sites"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Oxidation-Reduction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Models, Molecular"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Catalytic mechanism","Electron transfer","Flavin","Iron-sulfur Cluster","Electron Bifurcation"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Ferredoxins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Archaeal Proteins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2025.108410"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12018979"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12018979?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":3,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["pdb"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-05-15","dateOfCreation":"2025-03-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-23","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-03-18","firstPublicationDate":"2025-03-18","firstIndexDate":"2025-03-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-02","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"34589068","source":"MED","pmid":"34589068","pmcid":"PMC8473912","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC8473912"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2021.703525","title":"Energy Conservation in Fermentations of Anaerobic Bacteria.","authorString":"Buckel W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Buckel W","lastName":"Buckel","firstName":"Wolfgang","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratorium für Mikrobiologie, Fachbereich Biologie, Philipps-Universität Marburg, Marburg, Germany."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":3109445,"dateOfPublication":"2021 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2021,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Microbiol","title":"Frontiers in microbiology","essn":"1664-302X","issn":"1664-302X","nlmid":"101548977","isoabbreviation":"Front Microbiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2021-01-01"},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"703525","abstractText":"Anaerobic bacteria ferment carbohydrates and amino acids to obtain energy for growth. Due to the absence of oxygen and other inorganic electron acceptors, the substrate of a fermentation has to serve as electron donor as well as acceptor, which results in low free energies as compared to that of aerobic oxidations. Until about 10 years ago, anaerobes were thought to exclusively use substrate level phosphorylation (SLP), by which only part of the available energy could be conserved. Therefore, anaerobes were regarded as unproductive and inefficient energy conservers. The discovery of electrochemical Na<sup>+</sup> gradients generated by biotin-dependent decarboxylations or by reduction of NAD<sup>+</sup> with ferredoxin changed this view. Reduced ferredoxin is provided by oxidative decarboxylation of 2-oxoacids and the recently discovered flavin based electron bifurcation (FBEB). In this review, the two different fermentation pathways of glutamate to ammonia, CO<sub>2</sub>, acetate, butyrate and H<sub>2</sub> via 3-methylaspartate or via 2-hydroxyglutarate by members of the <i>Firmicutes</i> are discussed as prototypical examples in which all processes characteristic for fermentations occur. Though the fermentations proceed on two entirely different pathways, the maximum theoretical amount of ATP is conserved in each pathway. The occurrence of the 3-methylaspartate pathway in clostridia from soil and the 2-hydroxyglutarate pathway in the human microbiome of the large intestine is traced back to the oxygen-sensitivity of the radical enzymes. The coenzyme B<sub>12</sub>-dependent glutamate mutase in the 3-methylaspartate pathway tolerates oxygen, whereas 2-hydroxyglutaryl-CoA dehydratase is extremely oxygen-sensitive and can only survive in the gut, where the combustion of butyrate produced by the microbiome consumes the oxygen and provides a strict anaerobic environment. Examples of coenzyme B<sub>12</sub>-dependent eliminases are given, which in the gut are replaced by simpler extremely oxygen sensitive glycyl radical enzymes.","affiliation":"Laboratorium für Mikrobiologie, Fachbereich Biologie, Philipps-Universität Marburg, Marburg, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["ferredoxin","Decarboxylation","Electron Bifurcation","Oxygen Sensitivity","Coenzyme B12","Rnf","Glycyl Radical Enzymes","Δμna+"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2021.703525"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8473912"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8473912?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":66,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2021-09-30","dateOfRevision":"2021-10-01","electronicPublicationDate":"2021-09-13","firstPublicationDate":"2021-09-13","firstIndexDate":"2021-10-01","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"35179672","source":"MED","pmid":"35179672","pmcid":"PMC9307552","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC9307552"]},"doi":"10.1007/s10123-022-00234-z","title":"Biosynthesis of butyrate from methanol and carbon monoxide by recombinant Acetobacterium woodii.","authorString":"Chowdhury NP, Litty D, Müller V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chowdhury NP","lastName":"Chowdhury","firstName":"Nilanjan Pal","initials":"NP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology & Bioenergetics, Institute of Molecular Biosciences, Johann Wolfgang Goethe University, Max-von-Laue Str. 9, 60438, Frankfurt, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Litty D","lastName":"Litty","firstName":"Dennis","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology & Bioenergetics, Institute of Molecular Biosciences, Johann Wolfgang Goethe University, Max-von-Laue Str. 9, 60438, Frankfurt, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Müller V","lastName":"Müller","firstName":"Volker","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7955-5508"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology & Bioenergetics, Institute of Molecular Biosciences, Johann Wolfgang Goethe University, Max-von-Laue Str. 9, 60438, Frankfurt, Germany. vmueller@bio.uni-frankfurt.de."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7955-5508"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"25","journalIssueId":3394722,"dateOfPublication":"2022 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2022,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Int Microbiol","title":"International microbiology : the official journal of the Spanish Society for Microbiology","essn":"1618-1905","issn":"1139-6709","nlmid":"9816585","isoabbreviation":"Int Microbiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2022-08-01"},"pubYear":"2022","pageInfo":"551-560","abstractText":"Methanol is one of the most widely produced organic substrates from syngas and can serve as a bio-feedstock to cultivate acetogenic bacteria which allows a major contribution to reducing greenhouse gas. Acetobacterium woodii is one of the very few acetogens that can utilize methanol to produce acetate as sole product. Since A. woodii is genetically tractable, it is an interesting candidate to introduce recombinant pathways for production of bio-commodities from methanol. In this study, we introduced the butyrate production operon from a related acetogen, Eubacterium callanderi KIST612, into A. woodii and show a stable production of butyrate from methanol. This study also reveals how butyrate production by recombinant A. woodii strains can be enhanced with addition of electrons in the form of carbon monoxide. Our results not only show a stable expression system of non-native enzymes in A. woodii but also increase in the product spectrum of A. woodii to compounds with higher economic value.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology & Bioenergetics, Institute of Molecular Biosciences, Johann Wolfgang Goethe University, Max-von-Laue Str. 9, 60438, Frankfurt, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Johann Wolfgang Goethe-Universität, Frankfurt am Main","orderIn":0},{"agency":"European Research Council","grantId":"741791","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Acetobacterium","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Carbon Monoxide","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Methanol","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Butyrates","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Butyrate","Carbon cycling","bioengineering","Acetogenesis","Wood-ljungdahl Pathway","Acetogen"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Butyrates","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Methanol","registryNumber":"Y4S76JWI15"},{"name":"Carbon Monoxide","registryNumber":"7U1EE4V452"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10123-022-00234-z.pdf"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10123-022-00234-z.pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10123-022-00234-z"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9307552"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9307552?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":15,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2022-07-26","dateOfCreation":"2022-02-18","dateOfRevision":"2025-07-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2022-02-18","firstPublicationDate":"2022-02-18","firstIndexDate":"2022-02-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-13","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"36532432","source":"MED","pmid":"36532432","pmcid":"PMC9751042","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC9751042"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2022.1029886","title":"Looking for the mechanism of arsenate respiration of <i>Fusibacter</i> sp. strain 3D3, independent of ArrAB.","authorString":"Acosta-Grinok M, Vázquez S, Guiliani N, Marín S, Demergasso C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Acosta-Grinok M","lastName":"Acosta-Grinok","firstName":"Mauricio","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3091-1986"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biotechnology Center, Universidad Católica del Norte, Antofagasta, Chile."}]}},{"fullName":"Vázquez S","lastName":"Vázquez","firstName":"Susana","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0760-6254"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cátedra de Biotecnología, Facultad de Farmacia y Bioquímica, Universidad de Buenos Aires, Buenos Aires, Argentina."},{"affiliation":"Instituto de Nanobiotecnología (NANOBIOTEC), Universidad de Buenos Aires (UBA) - Consejo Nacional de Investigaciones Científicas y Técnicas (CONICET), Buenos Aires, Argentina."}]}},{"fullName":"Guiliani N","lastName":"Guiliani","firstName":"Nicolás","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9950-7316"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Departamento de Biología, Facultad de Ciencias, Universidad de Chile, Antofagasta, Chile."}]}},{"fullName":"Marín S","lastName":"Marín","firstName":"Sabrina","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biotechnology Center, Universidad Católica del Norte, Antofagasta, Chile."}]}},{"fullName":"Demergasso C","lastName":"Demergasso","firstName":"Cecilia","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4563-3066"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biotechnology Center, Universidad Católica del Norte, Antofagasta, Chile."},{"affiliation":"Nucleus for the Study of Cancer at a Basic, Applied, and Clinical Level, Universidad Católica del Norte, Antofagasta, Chile."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0760-6254"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3091-1986"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9950-7316"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4563-3066"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":3306974,"dateOfPublication":"2022 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2022,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Microbiol","title":"Frontiers in microbiology","essn":"1664-302X","issn":"1664-302X","nlmid":"101548977","isoabbreviation":"Front Microbiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2022-01-01"},"pubYear":"2022","pageInfo":"1029886","abstractText":"The literature has reported the isolation of arsenate-dependent growing microorganisms which lack a canonical homolog for respiratory arsenate reductase, ArrAB. We recently isolated an arsenate-dependent growing bacterium from volcanic arsenic-bearing environments in Northern Chile, <i>Fusibacter</i> sp. strain 3D3 (<i>Fas</i>) and studied the arsenic metabolism in this Gram-positive isolate. Features of <i>Fas</i> deduced from genome analysis and comparative analysis with other arsenate-reducing microorganisms revealed the lack of ArrAB coding genes and the occurrence of two <i>arsC</i> genes encoding for putative cytoplasmic arsenate reductases named ArsC-1 and ArsC-2. Interestingly, ArsC-1 and ArsC-2 belong to the thioredoxin-coupled family (because of the redox-active disulfide protein used as reductant), but they conferred differential arsenate resistance to the <i>E. coli</i> WC3110 Δ<i>arsC</i> strain. PCR experiments confirmed the absence of <i>arrAB</i> genes and results obtained using uncouplers revealed that <i>Fas</i> growth is linked to the proton gradient. In addition, <i>Fas</i> harbors ferredoxin-NAD<sup>+</sup> oxidoreductase (Rnf) and electron transfer flavoprotein (<i>etf</i>) coding genes. These are key molecular markers of a recently discovered flavin-based electron bifurcation mechanism involved in energy conservation, mainly in anaerobic metabolisms regulated by the cellular redox state and mostly associated with cytoplasmic enzyme complexes. At least three electron-bifurcating flavoenzyme complexes were evidenced in <i>Fas</i>, some of them shared in conserved genomic regions by other members of the <i>Fusibacter</i> genus. These physiological and genomic findings permit us to hypothesize the existence of an uncharacterized arsenate-dependent growth metabolism regulated by the cellular redox state in the <i>Fusibacter</i> genus.","affiliation":"Biotechnology Center, Universidad Católica del Norte, Antofagasta, Chile.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Thioredoxin","ferredoxin","Electron Bifurcation","Etf","Rnf Complex","Arsenic Respiration","Fusibacter","Northern Chile"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2022.1029886"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9751042"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9751042?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":6,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen","gca","refseq","uniprot"]},"dateOfCreation":"2022-12-19","dateOfRevision":"2023-01-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2022-12-01","firstPublicationDate":"2022-12-01","firstIndexDate":"2022-12-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"36073803","source":"MED","pmid":"36073803","pmcid":"PMC9600882","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC9600882"]},"doi":"10.1128/msystems.00534-22","title":"Revealing the Characteristics of Glucose- and Lactate-Based Chain Elongation for Caproate Production by <i>Caproicibacterium lactatifermentans</i> through Transcriptomic, Bioenergetic, and Regulatory Analyses.","authorString":"Wang H, Zhou W, Gao J, Ren C, Xu Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wang H","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Huilin","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lab of Brewing Microbiology and Applied Enzymology, Key Laboratory of Industrial Biotechnology of Ministry of Education, Jiangnan Universitygrid.258151.a, Wuxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou W","lastName":"Zhou","firstName":"Weicheng","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lab of Brewing Microbiology and Applied Enzymology, Key Laboratory of Industrial Biotechnology of Ministry of Education, Jiangnan Universitygrid.258151.a, Wuxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Gao J","lastName":"Gao","firstName":"Jiangjing","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lab of Brewing Microbiology and Applied Enzymology, Key Laboratory of Industrial Biotechnology of Ministry of Education, Jiangnan Universitygrid.258151.a, Wuxi, China."},{"affiliation":"The Center for Solid-state Fermentation Engineering of Anhui Province, Bozhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ren C","lastName":"Ren","firstName":"Cong","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lab of Brewing Microbiology and Applied Enzymology, Key Laboratory of Industrial Biotechnology of Ministry of Education, Jiangnan Universitygrid.258151.a, Wuxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu Y","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Yan","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7919-4762"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lab of Brewing Microbiology and Applied Enzymology, Key Laboratory of Industrial Biotechnology of Ministry of Education, Jiangnan Universitygrid.258151.a, Wuxi, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7919-4762"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"7","journalIssueId":3445209,"dateOfPublication":"2022 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2022,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"mSystems","title":"mSystems","essn":"2379-5077","issn":"2379-5077","nlmid":"101680636","isoabbreviation":"mSystems"},"printPublicationDate":"2022-10-01"},"pubYear":"2022","pageInfo":"e0053422","abstractText":"Caproate, an important medium-chain fatty acid, can only be synthesized by limited bacterial species by using ethanol, lactate, or certain saccharides. <i>Caproicibacterium lactatifermentans</i> is a promising caproate producer due to its glucose and lactate utilization capabilities. However, the global cellular responses of this bacterium to different carbon sources were not well understood. Here, <i>C. lactatifermentans</i> showed robust growth on glucose but more active caproate synthesis on lactate. Comparative transcriptome revealed that the genes involved in reverse β-oxidation for caproate synthesis and V-type ATPase-dependent ATP generation were upregulated under lactate condition, while several genes responsible for biomass synthesis were upregulated under glucose condition. Based on metabolic pathway reconstructions and bioenergetics analysis, the biomass accumulation on glucose condition may be supported by sufficient supplies of ATP and metabolite intermediates via glycolysis. In contrast, the ATP yield per glucose equivalent from lactate conversion into caproate was only 20% of that from glucose. Thus, the upregulation of the reverse β-oxidation genes may be essential for cell survival under lactate conditions. Furthermore, the remarkably decreased lactate utilization was observed after glucose acclimatization, indicating the negative modulation of lactate utilization by glucose metabolism. Based on the cotranscription of the lactate utilization repressor gene <i>lldR</i> with sugar-specific PTS genes and the opposite expression patterns of <i>lldR</i> and lactate utilization genes, a novel regulatory mechanism of glucose-repressed lactate utilization mediated via <i>lldR</i> was proposed. The results of this study suggested the molecular mechanism underlying differential physiologic and metabolic characteristics of <i>C. lactatifermentans</i> grown on glucose and lactate. <b>IMPORTANCE</b> <i>Caproicibacterium lactatifermentans</i> is a unique and robust caproate-producing bacterium in the family Oscillospiraceae due to its lactate utilization capability, whereas its close relatives such as <i>Caproicibacterium amylolyticum</i>, Caproiciproducens galactitolivorans, and <i>Caproicibacter fermentans</i> cannot utilize lactate but produce lactate as the main fermentation end product. Moreover, <i>C. lactatifermentans</i> can also utilize several saccharides such as glucose and maltose. Although the metabolic versatility of the bacterium makes it to be a promising industrial caproate producer, the cellular responses of <i>C. lactatifermentans</i> to different carbon sources were unknown. Here, the molecular mechanisms of biomass synthesis supported by glucose utilization and the cell survival supported by lactate utilization were revealed. A novel insight into the regulatory machinery in which glucose negatively regulates lactate utilization was proposed. This study provides a valuable basis to control and optimize caproate production, which will contribute to achieving a circular economy and environmental sustainability.","affiliation":"Lab of Brewing Microbiology and Applied Enzymology, Key Laboratory of Industrial Biotechnology of Ministry of Education, Jiangnan Universitygrid.258151.a, Wuxi, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Chinese Baijiu Industrial Technology Innovation Strategic Alliance","grantId":"2018-3","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"31530055","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"32172177","orderIn":0},{"agency":"China Postdoctoral Science Foundation","grantId":"2018M640454","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"21706097","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National First-Class Discipline Program of Light Industry Technology and Engineering","grantId":"LITE2018-12","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Caproates","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Lactic Acid","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Glucose"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adenosine Triphosphate","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Oxidation-Reduction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transcriptome","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_Y