naerobic Respiration With Protons (Ech) or NAD<sup>+</sup> (Rnf) as Electron Acceptors: A Historical Review.","authorString":"Buckel W, Thauer RK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Buckel W","lastName":"Buckel","firstName":"Wolfgang","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory for Microbiology, Faculty of Biology, Philipps-Universität Marburg, Marburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Thauer RK","lastName":"Thauer","firstName":"Rudolf K","initials":"RK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Max Planck Institute for Terrestrial Microbiology, Marburg, Germany."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":2645049,"dateOfPublication":"2018 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2018,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Microbiol","title":"Frontiers in microbiology","essn":"1664-302X","issn":"1664-302X","nlmid":"101548977","isoabbreviation":"Front Microbiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2018-01-01"},"pubYear":"2018","pageInfo":"401","abstractText":"Flavin-based electron bifurcation is a newly discovered mechanism, by which a hydride electron pair from NAD(P)H, coenzyme F<sub>420</sub>H<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>, or formate is split by flavoproteins into one-electron with a more negative reduction potential and one with a more positive reduction potential than that of the electron pair. Via this mechanism microorganisms generate low- potential electrons for the reduction of ferredoxins (Fd) and flavodoxins (Fld). The first example was described in 2008 when it was found that the butyryl-CoA dehydrogenase-electron-transferring flavoprotein complex (Bcd-EtfAB) of <i>Clostridium kluyveri</i> couples the endergonic reduction of ferredoxin (E<sub>0</sub>' = -420 mV) with NADH (-320 mV) to the exergonic reduction of crotonyl-CoA to butyryl-CoA (-10 mV) with NADH. The discovery was followed by the finding of an electron-bifurcating Fd- and NAD-dependent [FeFe]-hydrogenase (HydABC) in <i>Thermotoga maritima</i> (2009), Fd-dependent transhydrogenase (NfnAB) in various bacteria and archaea (2010), Fd- and H<sub>2</sub>-dependent heterodisulfide reductase (MvhADG-HdrABC) in methanogenic archaea (2011), Fd- and NADH-dependent caffeyl-CoA reductase (CarCDE) in <i>Acetobacterium woodii</i> (2013), Fd- and NAD-dependent formate dehydrogenase (HylABC-FdhF2) in <i>Clostridium acidi-urici</i> (2013), Fd- and NADP-dependent [FeFe]-hydrogenase (HytA-E) in <i>Clostridium autoethanogrenum</i> (2013), Fd(?)- and NADH-dependent methylene-tetrahydrofolate reductase (MetFV-HdrABC-MvhD) in <i>Moorella thermoacetica</i> (2014), Fd- and NAD-dependent lactate dehydrogenase (LctBCD) in <i>A. woodii</i> (2015), Fd- and F<sub>420</sub>H<sub>2</sub>-dependent heterodisulfide reductase (HdrA2B2C2) in <i>Methanosarcina acetivorans</i> (2017), and Fd- and NADH-dependent ubiquinol reductase (FixABCX) in <i>Azotobacter vinelandii</i> (2017). The electron-bifurcating flavoprotein complexes known to date fall into four groups that have evolved independently, namely those containing EtfAB (CarED, LctCB, FixBA) with bound FAD, a NuoF homolog (HydB, HytB, or HylB) harboring FMN, NfnB with bound FAD, or HdrA harboring FAD. All these flavoproteins are cytoplasmic except for the membrane-associated protein FixABCX. The organisms-in which they have been found-are strictly anaerobic microorganisms except for the aerobe <i>A. vinelandii</i>. The electron-bifurcating complexes are involved in a variety of processes such as butyric acid fermentation, methanogenesis, acetogenesis, anaerobic lactate oxidation, dissimilatory sulfate reduction, anaerobic- dearomatization, nitrogen fixation, and CO<sub>2</sub> fixation. They contribute to energy conservation via the energy-converting ferredoxin: NAD<sup>+</sup> reductase complex Rnf or the energy-converting ferredoxin-dependent hydrogenase complex Ech. This Review describes how this mechanism was discovered.","affiliation":"Laboratory for Microbiology, Faculty of Biology, Philipps-Universität Marburg, Marburg, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Max-Planck-Institut für Terrestrische Mikrobiologie","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["ferredoxin","Energy Conservation","Flavodoxin","Electron Bifurcation","Ech-complex","Rnf-complex","Crossed-over Reduction Potentials","Electron-transferring Flavoproteins (Etfab)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmicb.2018.00401/pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2018.00401"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC5861303"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC5861303?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":247,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2018-03-30","dateOfRevision":"2024-03-14","electronicPublicationDate":"2018-03-14","firstPublicationDate":"2018-03-14","firstIndexDate":"2018-03-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-03","hasEvaluations":"N"}]}}